<article class="env-lemma" id="0GUW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GUW">Lemma <span data-tag="0GUW">76.39.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to B$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $U \subset B$ be an open subspace. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$B$ is quasi-compact and quasi-separated, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$U$ is quasi-compact, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f : X \to B$ is proper, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f^{-1}(U) \to U$ is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then there exists a $U$-admissible blowup $B' \to B$ such that the strict transform $X'$ of $X$ maps isomorphically to $B'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/087E" data-tag="087E">76.39.1</a> we may assume that $X \to B$ is flat and of finite presentation. After replacing $B$ by a $U$-admissible blowup if necessary, we may assume that $U \subset B$ is scheme theoretically dense. Then $f$ is finite by Lemma <a href="/tag/0B4J" data-tag="0B4J">76.37.4</a> and an open immersion by Lemma <a href="/tag/0879" data-tag="0879">76.37.5</a>. Hence $f$ is an open immersion whose image is closed and contains the dense open $U$, whence $f$ is an isomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>